\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Working During School and Academic Performance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 15, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Previous research has attempted to measure the effect working during school
 has on academic performance.
 However, results have been mixed due to the biases that are potentially
 present.
 The number of hours that are worked is endogenously chosen by the individual;
 highly motivated people with strong ability tend to work more and perform
 better in school.
 In order to overcome the possible endogeneity problem, Stinebrickner and
 Stinebrickner (2003) used instrumental variable estimators to measure the
 true effect that youth employment has on educational outcomes.
 The authors data collected from Berea College where there is a mondatory
 work-study program.
 The IV estimator they obtained indicated that working additional hours
 has a harmful effect on academic performance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The IV approach the authors utilize was incredibly clean and well-executed,
 so there is every reason to believe that their results are internally valid.
 However, Beraea College is a unique institution, so the authors do not
 conclude that negative effects of the same magnitutde would necessarily
 exist in other youth employment contexts.
 Consequently, it is doubtful that the IV estimates that they provide have
 a high level of external validity.
 To combat this issue, I propose a regression discontinuity design that
 may have more far reaching implications than those presented in Stinebrickner
 and Stinebrickner (2003).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To receive federal student aid for college, students are required to fill
 out the free application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA).
 It is required for a student to provide personal financial information,
 and if a student is classified as a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

dependent,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then the financial information of his/her parents must also be provided.
 The application is then processed and passed along to the university of
 the student.
 On many occassions, universities request additional paperwork from students,
 such as tax returns from the students and/or parents, W-2 forms, etc., in
 order to verify that all the information provided on the submitted applications
 is valid.
 Finally, the universities allocate the available federal student aid based
 on criteria that corresponds with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

need.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Students who indicate interest in the federal work-study program may be
 assigned a job on the university campus to cover the additional costs of
 attendance that were unable to be covered by other forms of financial aid.
 Assignment into the federal work-study program is based on the financial
 needs of the student and his/her family.
 Those with a large amount of costs still needing to be covered will be
 granted the ability to enter into the work-study program.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, the situation just described offers me the opportunity to employ a regressio
n discontinuity approach to measure the effect working during school has
 on academic performance.
 To elaborate, those students with low remaining costs will not be assigned
 to the federal work-study program, while students with a high level of
 remaining costs will be.
 Most of these decisions are formulated using a certain pre-set threshold.
 Now, the necessary identifying assumption for a regression is that there
 is no manipulation around the cutoff.
 This means that all other determinants of the outcome vary smoothly across
 the threshold.
 While there may be some concerns about students and their families reporting
 true financial information, I do not believe this will not be a serious
 problem for my project.
 First off, individuals who fill out a FAFSA are generally unaware of the
 specific characteristics necessary to be eligible for financial aid, including
 being assigned to a work-study program.
 Next, universities have several thorough measures to determine whether
 the information they receive on an application is valid.
 Finally, misreporting financial data is a federal offense, so I am assuming
 that the possibility of getting caught, along with the threat of punishment,
 would deter a large amount of people from doing this.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moving on, just because a student is assigned to the federal work-study
 program does not mean he/she is required to accept.
 There are many students who may decline the offer to join the federal work-stud
y program.
 As a result, my setup will be a fuzzy RD, so the estimates I obtain will
 be a local average treatment effect that only will only apply to compliers.
 Nevertheless, the project can still provide notable insight since the data
 will be collected from a wide range of universities across the country,
 which will be chosen randomly.
 As long as identities are kept anonymous, then I am fairly certain I can
 obtain the necessary data regarding federal work-study decisions, as well
 as educational outcomes.
 Since this a regression discontinuity setup, I will not require any information
 that actually identifies a particular student because the cross-section
 approach is irrelevant.
 The only feature that needs to be ascertained is whether or not a student
 was assigned to the federal work-study program and if he/she chose to accpet.
\end_layout

